Systemd Timer

IN RHEL 7, the introduction of systemd brought new feature called systemd timer units. these timer units trigger another unit, like a service, whose name corresponds to the timer unit's name. the timer units allow timer based activation of other units. for easier debugging, systemd logs timer events in system journals

the sysstat package provides a systemd timer unit called sysstat-collect.timer to collect statistics every 10minutes

A systemd timer is a modern Linux utility used to schedule tasks and automate scripts. It serves as the primary native alternative to the traditional cron utility on modern, systemd-based Linux distributions.
Unlike cron, which directly runs a command at a set time, a systemd timer works as an "alarm clock" that triggers a separate systemd service, which then executes the actual task.

How it Works: The Two-File Pairing

To set up a systemd timer, you must create two distinct files in /etc/systemd/system/ that share the exact same base name:
1. The Service File (.service): Defines what command or script to run.
2. The Timer File (.timer): Defines when or how often to run it.

Step 1: Create the Service (/etc/systemd/system/myjob.service)
systemd service file configuration

This is a standard service file, usually configured as a oneshot type since scheduled tasks typically run and finish.

init
[Unit]
Description=My Automated Backup Script

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/mybackup.sh
Use code with caution.


Key Characteristics of ExecStart

• Requires Full Paths: systemd does not read your user shell profile or $PATH variables. You must always provide the absolute path to the executable (e.g., /usr/bin/python3 instead of just python3).
• Defines the Main Process: Unless configured as a background/forking process (Type=forking), the process launched by ExecStart is treated by the system as the main process of that service.
• Strict Constraints: For standard services, exactly one ExecStart directive is allowed. The only exception is when Type=oneshot is specified, which allows you to list multiple ExecStart lines that run sequentially.

