Step 2: Create the Timer (/etc/systemd/system/myjob.timer)

The timer file links to the service by name automatically and holds the schedule.
ini
[Unit]
Description=Run My Automated Backup Script Daily

[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
Use code with caution.

Types of Schedules

Systemd timers allow for two powerful types of scheduling:
1. Realtime Timers (Wall-clock): These trigger tasks at specific dates or calendar times, exactly like cron.
	• Example: OnCalendar=Mon..Fri *-*-* 10:00:00 (Runs every weekday at 10:00 AM).
2. Monotonic Timers: These trigger tasks relative to system events or time elapsed, which cron cannot naturally do.
	• OnBootSec=15min (Runs 15 minutes after the system boots).
	• OnUnitActiveSec=1h (Runs 1 hour after the last time the service was activated).

Key Advantages Over Cron


Feature	Systemd Timers	Traditional Cron
Missed Job Recovery	Yes (Persistent=true). If the PC was powered off during the scheduled time, it catches up immediately upon boot.	No. (Requires a separate utility like anacron).
Logging & Debugging	Centralized. All execution output and errors are captured neatly in journalctl.	Fragmented. Outputs are either emailed locally or buried in /var/log/syslog.
Dependencies	Yes. You can make a timer wait until a network connection or specific service is active.	No. Cron fires blindly, regardless of the system's state.
Resource Control	Granular. Easily restrict CPU, RAM, or I/O priority for the task using standard systemd directives.	Complex. Requires prefixing commands with utilities like nice or ionice.


Essential Commands for Managing Timers

Once you write or modify your unit files, you control them using systemctl:
• Reload the systemd daemon (do this every time you create/edit a file):bash
